body:
@alexanderadam `HttpOnly` option is implemented since #4706.
However, according to [Mozilla Observatory](https://observatory.mozilla.org/), it seems to be the only one:
![cookie_gitea](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1255977/77965018-75a1cc00-72e0-11ea-9775-9cf611813156.png)

While waiting for these flags to be set at the application level, I found a workaround which consists in setting cookie flags at the web server level. For instance with `nginx`, one could adapt the [configuration sample](https://docs.gitea.io/en-us/reverse-proxies/) provided by the documentation by using the [proxy_cookie_path](http://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_cookie_path) directive as such:

```
server {
    (...)

    location / {
        # Workaround to set cookie flags:
        proxy_cookie_path / "/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=lax";

        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
    }
}
```

Which results in:
![cookie_gitea2](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1255977/78503581-e37a4780-7767-11ea-898f-4fba745f1860.png)


If your instance of `gitea` is supposed to be accessed with an unsecure channel (HTTP), remove the `Secure` flag. I also set `SameSite` to `Lax` which provides a reasonable level of protection. `Strict` mode implies higher security rules but it might have potential drawbacks since it prevents the browser from attaching any cookies to cross-site requests. I do not know `gitea` enough to determine if it could be a problem.
